          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"balzac-illusions-perdues-02f3d2e81d2ecdde\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.91,\"analysis\":\"Coralie’s “Je t’aimerai mieux en ne te trompant point” primarily presents as true a future loving attitude toward Camusot: she will love him better once she is no longer deceiving him. In conjunction with her invitation to continue visiting, the future tense also gives the avowal a moderate promise-like or relationally undertaking force. Nothing indicates that the words are a reflexive affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future form can function both as a prediction of future feeling and as an assurance or undertaking. “Mieux” may mean loving him better in quality rather than loving him more intensely.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Coralie directly addresses Camusot with a future-tense love avowal qualified by her promise of no longer deceiving him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Coralie has openly chosen Lucien while allowing Camusot to keep visiting and retaining the material arrangement he supports. Her statement offers Camusot a more honest, qualified form of affection rather than exclusive romantic fidelity. Its potentially consoling or instrumental role does not alter its predominantly truth-conditional and moderately undertaking force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Viens, mon pauvre Musot, viens tant que tu voudras, dit-elle. Je t'aimerai mieux en ne te trompant point.”\",\"supports\":\"The direct address and future love clause support a prospective avowal, while the invitation and honesty condition add moderate relational-undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Coralie declares that she loves Lucien madly, while Camusot asks to retain the right to visit her.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance offers Camusot continued but nonexclusive affection within a newly explicit relational arrangement.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration presents Coralie’s reply as composed dialogue and supplies no indication that she blurts out or involuntarily discharges the words.\",\"supports\":\"Absence of positive evidence for expressive/reflexive core force; emotional complexity alone does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative future tense, syntactic family “main_or_embedded.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the prospective form relevant to both truth-conditional prediction and possible promise-like force.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and moderate P naturally capture the prospective avowal and its assurance-like role. The surrounding triangle, possible calculation, and compromised sincerity are contextual matters rather than missing dimensions of core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
